#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054574 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "ae9cba85-b0ed-59c9-bf9f-682b921c5ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6258" :question/problem "Given that $(x-1)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of the polynomial $f(x)= x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6,$ factor $f(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6}{x-1} = x^3-7x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x^3-7x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3-7x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3} = x^2+3x +2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x^2+3x +2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x+2)(x+1).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048497}] :knowledge-point/question-gener