n Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140a15bc-fcf1-5cd5-bb3c-e84afc03fb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x-1)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244496} {:answer/id #uuid "29f5e660-86ea-5256-8c05-d4e7429d46d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x+1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244687} {:answer/id #uuid "4978aa35-7d7f-5b59-8af9-843564a32b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+1)(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244967} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5f3ae5-6893-5186-b94f-2e8294ab9c51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-1)(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245994} {:answer/id #uuid "bce4f31e-2daa-5469-ae29-c4736cf33aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce4f31e-2daa-5469-ae29-c4736cf33aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245999} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5d1fe0-66e0-5072-aedc-5e9a7b18459a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "904dff05-ecb6-51b3-917d-26bcf8537c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-112615" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $-13$ | $7$ | $17$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-12$ | $-15$ | $6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-7$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2}{x+1}=3{x}^{2}-7x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)(3{x}^{2}-7x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1){{(3x-1)(x-2)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-1)(x-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b24578e-481e-553a-a83a-dc64b6635d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244416} {:answer/id #uuid "39f82396-0c2d-56e1-b9fd-de4a09dd64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244827} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcb12f3-3780-53db-adc2-7c2e38923858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-3)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245004} {:answer/id #uuid "b902a4ce-fd1e-5700-be25-6073ff3196a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x+2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245966} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb9253d-1f88-5f8c-9b58-b795ed407dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x-3)(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f82396-0c2d-56e1-b9fd-de4a09dd64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244827} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ae9a65-2c31-50dc-9b0c-6b6e3de50ea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4055097-e27e-5747-8711-3ff850d11a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-112617" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $4$ | $-37$ | $-14$ | $24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $39$ | $6$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-12$ | $-4$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8}{x+4}=3{x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)(3{x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4){{(3x-2)(x+1)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-2)(x+1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292959fc-5935-574c-9f04-13e8671cf522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-7)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244674} {:answer/id #uuid "36ddaff0-33e2-5585-b7bd-e8cc98dc6e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244800} {:answer/id #uuid "45503d60-7034-5dc8-94fc-0693fe24418a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(5-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244928} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbc7b91-18d2-5c35-b07b-b9f5fa63305c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-x)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246129} {:answer/id #uuid "f620a82c-3ecf-57d2-9154-b09ad9b1016b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ddaff0-33e2-5585-b7bd-e8cc98dc6e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244800} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c5333b-8831-529d-9a73-a9a967c8862b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c8eab9f-7b10-5db7-a80e-51f13f149526" :question/math-academy-id "q-112626" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35.$ We know that $(x-5)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\\\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-36$ | $-2$ | $35$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $35$ | $-5$ | $-35$ |\n|  | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35}{x-5}={x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $7$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7}{x+1}={x}^{2}+6x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)({x}^{2}+6x-7).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1){{(x-1)(x+7)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x-1)(x+7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18490f60-4edc-508c-abe1-e7e0f13c065d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285003} {:answer/id #uuid "312ee193-d577-5512-a0e8-1cc54605d5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285231} {:answer/id #uuid "e405d079-ad9d-55f2-b5eb-d4d63e4f65f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286871} {:answer/id #uuid "eb434cbb-a7e4-590f-89bc-63a9f59cf077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286942} {:answer/id #uuid "f66903c6-f94c-504b-bfea-93036d198d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-x)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "312ee193-d577-5512-a0e8-1cc54605d5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285231} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ecf3453-145b-5f3a-bf2b-36b631e0d173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a78f6b62-d8f4-5963-b2aa-dcc684d0394b" :question/math-academy-id "q-18862" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-11$ | $9$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $-15$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18}{x-3}={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-3)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-2)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $8$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6}{x-2}={x}^{2}+4x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+4x+3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3){{(x+1)(x+3)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x+1)(x+3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc6878dd-7880-56e1-b90f-bffe1457db87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc6878dd-7880-56e1-b90f-bffe1457db87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304039} :answer-field/id #uuid "65363172-90a7-5c3c-8285-eb6ba0e5a62c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c8e3cf5-7e90-5470-a693-ef40ffd528ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-328505" :question/problem "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12\n$$\n\n \ncan be expressed as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-3)\\cdot P(x).\n$$\n\nFind the polynomial $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $16$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-10$ | $12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12}{x-2}={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6}{x-3}={x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x)={x}^{2}+x-2=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263727d6-d29d-5bf3-b0ce-64f4c4babbe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4 x - 2)(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518549} {:answer/id #uuid "34dd07d8-0fb5-5860-b429-c5d95674c1e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x + 2)(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518551} {:answer/id #uuid "632410d0-6cb4-56f4-acbe-ce18db1f0810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 2)(x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518556} {:answer/id #uuid "9c538ed4-e63a-59ea-b5d7-1bf260ff251e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x - 2)(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518566} {:answer/id #uuid "cf48ea9f-6f25-5c77-b923-06e53622bb21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x + 2)(x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "632410d0-6cb4-56f4-acbe-ce18db1f0810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 2)(x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518556} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c1f5d50-c28c-5707-9506-4115f417afd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0419dbc0-1445-5bc9-b917-8b83592d2040" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(x - 1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4.$ We know that $(x - 2)$ and $(x - 1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $-14$ | $23$ | $-16$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $14$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-8$ | $7$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4}{x - 2}=3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x - 1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x - 1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-8$ | $7$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{3} - 8 x^{2} + 7 x - 2}{x - 1}=3 x^{2} - 5 x + 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(x - 1)(3 x^{2} - 5 x + 2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 14 x^{3} + 23 x^{2} - 16 x + 4=(x - 2)(x - 1)\\underbrace{(3 x - 2)(x - 1)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3 x - 2)(x - 1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118ff845-c36f-5dd4-b4be-4d2f3371534c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(3 - x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518547} {:answer/id #uuid "78bfce02-698c-5299-8a19-d1bf108af9a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 3)(x + 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518558} {:answer/id #uuid "841dff1d-35b4-5aa5-8546-c8d913c38da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 8)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518559} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ae0968-bc81-5c09-a845-ff58e0c7403c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 - x)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518568} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c6aa70-6d67-5a46-a185-c447bb5eb7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(x + 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3c6aa70-6d67-5a46-a185-c447bb5eb7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(x + 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518574} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4776a5-a31b-5b77-b1f4-5f3410bc9a46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cc14d2b-da71-5a68-9167-1fb9cf53907c" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x - 1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48.$ We know that $(x - 3)$ and $(x - 1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-37$ | $82$ | $-48$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $15$ | $-66$ | $48$ |\n|  | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48}{x - 3}=x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x - 1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x - 1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 22 x + 16}{x - 1}=x^{2} + 6 x - 16,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x - 1)(x^{2} + 6 x - 16).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 2 x^{3} - 37 x^{2} + 82 x - 48=(x - 3)(x - 1)\\underbrace{(x - 2)(x + 8)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x - 2)(x + 8).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8f4cd9-343f-53f9-8da8-6987fb92aa94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518550} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5c0465-6f7c-51a9-b3cb-19ece0fb2e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)(x - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518552} {:answer/id #uuid "5f60ea94-d78d-524d-860b-1089e09972ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(x - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518555} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b189d5-4fe1-5252-a78f-94a54754ba0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518577} {:answer/id #uuid "eef6d77e-1cb5-5f09-87d0-24953d96ba59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 - x)(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5b189d5-4fe1-5252-a78f-94a54754ba0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518577} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9a23eb-f99e-5776-9814-77b6f94072b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "996d568b-7577-52b3-ac06-045db9647695" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 4)(x + 3)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96.$ We know that $(x + 4)$ and $(x + 3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x + 3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x + 3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x + 3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $13$ | $62$ | $128$ | $96$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $-3$ | $-30$ | $-96$ | $-96$ |\n|  | $1$ | $10$ | $32$ | $32$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96}{x + 3}=x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 3)(x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x + 4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x + 4)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $10$ | $32$ | $32$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $-24$ | $-32$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^{3} + 10 x^{2} + 32 x + 32}{x + 4}=x^{2} + 6 x + 8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 4)(x + 3)(x^{2} + 6 x + 8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^{4} + 13 x^{3} + 62 x^{2} + 128 x + 96=(x + 4)(x + 3)\\underbrace{(x + 2)(x + 4)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x + 2)(x + 4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a850ce8-6c2f-56e9-97a6-d54695d622f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4 x - 2)(x + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518553} {:answer/id #uuid "8f11b303-bb92-5db1-95e6-fc4409a201ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x + 2)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518562} {:answer/id #uuid "99dac682-5f1b-579c-8e61-9f4cda6acd23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x + 1)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518565} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ee89c0-7563-5d3c-aabb-4556e6f4b071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x - 2)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518571} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e6fe5f-e80f-5c47-9d4c-f222856f886d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 1)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8e6fe5f-e80f-5c47-9d4c-f222856f886d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 1)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518572} :answer-field/id #uuid "b57ab9bd-7e2d-5b40-9052-da97505a6cff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de6b8b43-d602-5cd2-be14-bed17971288f" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(x - 3)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6.$ We know that $(x - 1)$ and $(x - 3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-7$ | $-13$ | $23$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-17$ | $6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ | $-17$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6}{x - 1}=3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x - 3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x - 3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-17$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $9$ | $15$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} - 17 x + 6}{x - 3}=3 x^{2} + 5 x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(x - 3)(3 x^{2} + 5 x - 2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 7 x^{3} - 13 x^{2} + 23 x - 6=(x - 1)(x - 3)\\underbrace{(3 x - 1)(x + 2)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3 x - 1)(x + 2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a93499-f229-5ff0-9ba6-3bde21bfc3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x + 2)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518545} {:answer/id #uuid "20c38d13-4072-56a0-9b5d-ac570ea86a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 2)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518548} {:answer/id #uuid "9901435b-c59a-5204-b93c-abef284df7ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4 x - 2)(x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518563} {:answer/id #uuid "bec70bb8-e641-5a2a-909a-a7db4ae9cf80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x + 2)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518570} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8b82e6-c307-5894-ba37-88a78965716f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x - 2)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20c38d13-4072-56a0-9b5d-ac570ea86a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 x - 2)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518548} :answer-field/id #uuid "872fae21-720c-5b0a-b5fa-d5ec9c29c2ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e21b7f4a-96d6-5518-b504-9f9fc1a33098" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(x + 1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16.$ We know that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^{4}$ | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-17$ | $16$ | $20$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $12$ | $-20$ | $-16$ | $16$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16}{x - 4}=3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x + 1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x + 1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^{3}$ | $x^{2}$ | $x^{1}$ | $x^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\downarrow$ | $-3$ | $8$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-8$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 x^{3} - 5 x^{2} - 4 x + 4}{x + 1}=3 x^{2} - 8 x + 4,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(x + 1)(3 x^{2} - 8 x + 4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3 x^{4} - 17 x^{3} + 16 x^{2} + 20 x - 16=(x - 4)(x + 1)\\underbrace{(3 x - 2)(x - 2)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3 x - 2)(x - 2).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}]}
